.TH HASHMAP_DELETE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
hashmap_delete \- Delete a key from a hashmap

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/hashmap.h>

.IB void
.IB hashmap_delete(hashmap* map, const char* key);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBhashmap_delete\fR function deletes a key and its associated value from a hashmap. It takes a pointer to the hashmap and the key as arguments.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB hashmap*
A pointer to the hashmap.
.IB const char*
The key to be deleted.

The function does not return any value.

The \fBhashmap_delete\fR function is intended to be used to remove entries from a hashmap when they are no longer needed.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBhashmap_delete\fR function to delete a key from a hashmap:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/hashmap.h>
#include <lib/str.h>

int main() {
    hashmap* map = hashmap_new();
    const char* key = "example";

    hashmap_set(map, key, "value");

    printf("Value before deletion: %s\n", (char*)hashmap_get(map, key));

    hashmap_delete(map, key);

    printf("Value after deletion: %s\n", (char*)hashmap_get(map, key));

    hashmap_free(map);

    return 0;
}
.nef